KZ3 patch 2205 (RSX pass 174): RSX_PERF_DRAW=1 sampled texture-loop split, [texloop-split] (diagnostic, no new switch; RSX_PERF_DRAW off = unchanged).
Pass 127's timing left the loop's cost unlocated (the RE notes, pass 127: "ASSUMPTION: it sits in the surface/zeta branches
(zdepth_snapshot copies, surface_window, ld_surface_rg16) and the QPC pairs; sub-timers there decide"). This adds those sub-timers and
cuts the cost of the timers themselves by sampling: rsx_texloop_split.h (pure C) times one draw in each block of 16 (the draw counter and a fixed-seed
generator pick its offset in the block, so the sample cannot alias with a draw pattern whose period divides 16; set inside ld_pd_draw around the draw) and the report scales each sampled sum by draws / sampled, so every row reads as us per draw over
all draws, comparable to the [perf-draw] rows. Buckets (leaves, none nested): enabled units (count), row check, register decode, sampler
key+slot, plain entry bind (texture_srv_slot and _entry at the three sites), zdepth_snapshot, surface_window, ld_surface_rg16, descriptor
writes (srv_table + sampler_table, after the loop), whole loop; "other" = loop minus the leaves from row check to rg16. Printed with the
[perf-draw] report as `[texloop-split]` lines. A non-sampled draw pays one int test per site and reads no clock; outside RSX_PERF_DRAW
the flag is never set. Host test tools/smoke/test_texloop_split.sh (synthetic draw loop, negative controls).
Cost of the probe (not removable): a sampled draw pays ~18 clock reads (~0.5 us) inside [perf-draw]'s t1 span, so its mean/p95 move slightly
(1 draw in 16), and every leaf carries its own timer pair, so "other" is an upper bound. The glue in rsx_live_draw.c is checked by source greps
and mingw -fsyntax-only, not run: only the pure header is host-tested.

